  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 027: A pellicle keeps particles off the chip's master pattern, helping prevent the same defect from appearing on many dies.

Lesson 028: An EUV mask is a precision mirror with a pattern that controls where light reaches the wafer.

Lesson 029: Inspecting and repairing masks helps stop a small error in a master pattern from becoming a large manufacturing loss.

Lesson 030: Mask writers create the patterns used in lithography, and their speed affects how quickly new designs can enter production.

Lesson 031: More chips only create more useful AI when memory, packaging, data centers and software can turn that extra supply into working systems.

The big idea

A finished accelerator die is a piece of silicon containing circuits. An accelerator package is the physical assembly that connects that die to memory, power and the rest of the computer.

Packaging is therefore part of the architecture. It influences which components can communicate, how densely they fit together and whether the product can be manufactured reliably.

Where we are

Fabricated and tested dies + separately manufactured memory → advanced package → server board → networked, powered cluster

1. Open the package in your mind

Picture a compute die with several memory stacks beside it. Underneath is a structure with very fine wiring that connects them. Beneath that is a package substrate, which provides connections at a scale suitable for the larger circuit board.

The fine wiring structure is called an interposer. The substrate is another layer of the assembly; the two words do not mean the same thing. Their exact materials and construction vary by packaging technology.

The practical problem is connecting a huge number of signals in a small space while delivering power and managing heat and mechanical stress.

2. What CoWoS actually does

CoWoS stands for Chip-on-Wafer-on-Substrate. It is TSMC's family of advanced packaging technologies for integrating compute dies and high-bandwidth memory.

CoWoS-S uses a silicon interposer. CoWoS-R uses a redistribution-layer interposer, built with polymer and copper routing. CoWoS-L combines redistribution wiring with local silicon interconnects. These are different ways to provide connections and scale the assembly; the family should not be treated as one identical production line.

For now, understand the function. We will revisit detailed routing and bonding when we can evaluate their system tradeoffs.

3. Why put compute and memory together?

A processor continually needs data. If moving that data becomes too restrictive, adding more arithmetic units may not help.

Advanced packaging makes it possible to place wide memory interfaces close to the logic and connect them densely. TSMC describes this integration as a way to improve system performance, efficiency and form factor.

The engineering question is no longer only how many transistors fit on one die. It also becomes how effectively several pieces of silicon work together.

This is your first link from the wafer fab to runtime performance: manufacturing choices determine the physical communication paths available to the software.

4. Why chiplets enter the picture

A chiplet is a die designed to operate as part of a larger multi-die product. Splitting functions can allow some blocks to use an advanced process and others to use a more mature process.

Recall Lesson 017: die size and defects affect manufacturing economics. Smaller dies can help in some circumstances, but splitting a processor also adds connections, design work and assembly requirements.

